[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Multi-Version Concurrency Control (MVCC) and Read Views in MySQL"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Multi-Version Concurrency Control (MVCC) is a fundamental database concurrency control mechanism used primarily by the InnoDB storage engine in MySQL. It allows multiple transactions to read and write simultaneously without unnecessarily locking each other out, thereby significantly improving database throughput and read performance."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Components of MVCC in InnoDB"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To implement MVCC, InnoDB maintains historical versions of data rows. Every table created in InnoDB includes three hidden fields appended to each row:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"DB_TRX_ID (6 bytes): Stores the identifier of the last transaction that inserted or updated the row. Deletions are also treated as updates with a hidden deletion flag."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"DB_ROLL_PTR (7 bytes): The roll pointer points to an undo log record. If the row was updated, the undo log contains the older version of the row necessary to reconstruct it."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"DB_ROW_ID (6 bytes): Contains a row ID that increases sequentially as rows are inserted. If InnoDB generates a clustered index automatically, this ID serves as the index."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Read Views"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Read View is a snapshot of the database state taken at the moment a consistent read (SELECT statement) starts under the REPEATABLE READ or READ COMMITTED isolation levels. The Read View contains critical transactional state information used by InnoDB to determine which version of a row a transaction is allowed to see."},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Read View primarily consists of the following components:"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"m_ids: A list of active, uncommitted transaction IDs at the time the Read View was created."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"min_trx_id: The lowest transaction ID among the active transactions in m_ids."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"max_trx_id: The transaction ID that will be assigned to the next transaction to be created (the highest ID + 1)."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"creator_trx_id: The transaction ID of the transaction that created the Read View."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Row Visibility Algorithm"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a transaction executes a query and evaluates a row with a specific DB_TRX_ID, InnoDB applies the following visibility rules:"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"1. If DB_TRX_ID < min_trx_id, the transaction that modified the row committed before the Read View was created. The row version is visible."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"2. If DB_TRX_ID >= max_trx_id, the transaction that modified the row was created after the Read View. The row version is NOT visible; InnoDB must follow the DB_ROLL_PTR to read an older undo log version."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"3. If DB_TRX_ID is between min_trx_id and max_trx_id, check if DB_TRX_ID is in the m_ids list. If it is in the list, the transaction was still active when the Read View was created, so the row is NOT visible. If it is not in the list, the transaction had already committed, and the row IS visible."},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Practical Example: Isolation Levels"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"The creation timing of Read Views dictates the behavior of isolation levels in MySQL InnoDB:"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"READ COMMITTED: A new Read View is created for EVERY consistent read statement executed within the transaction. This allows the transaction to see data committed by other concurrent transactions."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"REPEATABLE READ: A Read View is created only once when the FIRST consistent read is performed in the transaction. All subsequent reads in the same transaction use this exact same Read View, ensuring repeatable reads."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Demonstrating MVCC in MySQL"},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"CREATE TABLE accounts (id INT PRIMARY KEY, balance INT) ENGINE=InnoDB;
INSERT INTO accounts VALUES (1, 100);

-- Transaction 1
START TRANSACTION;
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1; -- Returns 100

-- Concurrently, Transaction 2 executes:
UPDATE accounts SET balance = 200 WHERE id = 1;
COMMIT;

-- Back to Transaction 1 (under REPEATABLE READ):
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1; -- Still returns 100 due to the initial Read View!"},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"MVCC and Read Views form the backbone of MySQL InnoDB's concurrency model. By keeping track of transaction IDs, undo logs, and point-in-time Read Views, MySQL delivers high performance without locking readers out during write operations."}]
